;; ADR-2608310700 — com-junkawasaki/root. EDN tx-data (ADR-2607171600): read with
;; (d/transact conn (edn/read-string (slurp "90-docs/adr/2608310700-....edn")))
(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608310700-the-game-dev-detector-offered-eleven-games-it-could-not-fix")
  (:adr/title "The game-dev detector put eleven unfixable games at the head of its candidate list")
  (:adr/status :accepted)
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608136000-six-questions-before-believing-a-green"
                "network-isekai/adr-0084-the-touch-recipe-does-not-reach-an-action-only-game"
                "network-isekai/adr-0085-the-itonami-depot-loop-shares-one-touch-shape"
                "network-isekai/adr-0086-care-coordination-and-the-gate-that-measured-two-teleports"))
  (:adr/body
  "CONTEXT

`scripts/isekai-game-dev-tick.cljs` is the measuring half of the isekai game-production
loop: it is deterministic, has no model in it, and the loop consumes its candidate
top-down. Its `mobile-input-gap` scan is two string conditions on the guest — logic.cljc
mentions `key-pressed?` and never mentions `(axis ` — which is a correct description of
a guest that a thumb cannot reach, because kami.input's pointer handlers call only
`on-axes`/`on-pointer` and `on-action` fires from `keydown`/`keyup` alone.

It is not a description of a guest that a thumb COULD reach if someone edited it. Those
are different questions, and the detector answered only the first while the loop read
the answer as the second.

network-isekai's ADR-0084 measured the difference on 2026-08-30 and recorded closing it
under NOT DONE: 'the action-only games remain unreachable by touch and should be
reported as such rather than offered as stick candidates.' The detector was not changed.
Today's tick offered `gftd/ghosthacker-duet` — the same game ADR-0084 was written about,
a rhythm loop whose entire input is `:actions {:hit #{\" \"}}`, whose scene declares
`:axes {}` and whose `:world :player-speed` is 0.0.

This is the shape ADR-2608136000 question 4 names: 'can the output tell skipped from
passed?' Here it is 'can the output tell needs-the-recipe from cannot-have-it?' It could
not — both were the same row, and alphabetical ordering put every unfixable one first
(`gftd/ghosthacker-*` and `gftd/quantum-excitation` sort before `itonami/*`).

The cost was recurring, not hypothetical. The three iterations that produced ADR-0084,
0085 and 0086 each read past the same block by hand before starting work. The trap is
live for a fresh-context agent: the skill's recipe applied to a rhythm chart has only
two outcomes, inventing on-screen buttons (art direction, which ADR-0083 and ADR-0084
both declined) or spending the round re-deriving ADR-0084.

DECISION 1 — the detector decides reachability from the scene, mirroring kami.input.

`touch-reachability` reads scene.edn with the EDN reader and reproduces the condition
under which `kami.input/sticks` returns no stick at all (measured at source today,
orgs/kotoba-lang/host src/kami/input.cljc L50-58): a stick exists if `:sticks` is
declared, or `:stick` is present, or both \"MoveX\" and \"MoveY\" are keys of `:axes`.
Otherwise the pointer emits nothing and no amount of guest editing reaches it.

  :both / :declared / :incomplete  -> candidate. `:incomplete` (one axis, so no stick
      is produced) is the drive shape ADR-0083 fixed by declaring the other axis; it is
      reachable, just not by the guest half alone.
  :none        -> reported as `:touch-unreachable`, never offered.
  :unmeasured  -> reported separately.

Candidates are ordered both < declared < incomplete, under the existing owner-reported
precedence, so the shape with the most worked examples comes first.

DECISION 2 — the unreachable games are reported every tick, not dropped.

`:touch-unreachable` carries the count, the game list and why. Filtering them into
silence would trade one false statement ('these need a stick') for another ('no game is
unplayable by touch'). The gap is real and stays visible; what changed is that the loop
is no longer told to close it by editing a game. Closing it needs a pointer-to-action
path in kami.input — a kotoba-lang/host change plus a pin advance, which ADR-0084
already declined to land from inside a game-production iteration for the same reason it
is declined here: it would land an input primitive with no owner.

DECISION 3 — a scene that could not be read is not a scene with no axes.

An unreadable or unparseable scene returns `:unmeasured` with the reason
(`:scene-unreadable` / `:scene-parse-error` / `:scene-not-a-map`), not `:none`. Folding
it into `:none` would drop a game out of the candidate list for a reason that was never
measured — the first of ADR-2608136000's six questions, in the direction that hides work
rather than inventing it.

EVIDENCE

Both directions measured against a synthetic repo (`/tmp/igd-probe`) driven through the
real script by `COM_JUNKAWASAKI_ROOT`, with eight games hand-built to sit one per
verdict. Post-fix, each row lands in its bucket for the reason it names:

  t/both          -> :both, candidate
  t/declared      -> :declared, candidate (`:axes {}` plus an explicit `:sticks`)
  t/incomplete    -> :incomplete, candidate, `:declared-axes [\"MoveX\"]`
  t/none          -> :touch-unreachable, not a candidate
  t/badscene      -> :unmeasured :scene-parse-error
  t/nofile        -> :unmeasured :scene-unreadable
  t/reads-axis    -> absent (reads `(axis `)
  t/no-keypressed -> absent (no `key-pressed?`)

Pre-fix, the same eight games produce six undifferentiated rows and the offered
candidate is `t/badscene` — a game whose scene could not be parsed at all. That is the
strongest statement of the defect: the instrument's first recommendation was a game it
had failed to read.

On the real repo the candidate moves from `gftd/ghosthacker-duet` to
`itonami/carpet-care`, and 11 games move to `:touch-unreachable`. The split is exact —
11 rows declare no axes and all have `:player-speed 0.0`; 7 declare both MoveX and
MoveY; no row is `:incomplete` or `:unmeasured` today.

CORRECTION TO ADR-0084

ADR-0084 counted '12 games ... the 11 ghosthacker titles and gftd/quantum-excitation'.
Measured today there are 10 ghosthacker titles, so the action-only class is 11, not 12,
and its own two figures did not agree with each other (11 + 1 = 12 was also reported
alongside a 21-game total that only reconciles at 11). Its 21-game total is consistent
with 11 action-only plus 10 steering (goriketsu and nine itonami titles), of which three
have since been fixed, leaving today's 18. The reasoning ADR-0084 recorded is unaffected;
the count is corrected here rather than in that ADR because the arithmetic is the only
part that moved and the ADR's own decisions still stand as written.

NOT DONE

- No pointer-to-action path in kami.input. The 11 action-only games are still unplayable
  by touch; this change makes the loop say so instead of pointing at them.
- No game changed in this iteration. The production increment this round is the
  instrument, not a title. `itonami/carpet-care` is the next candidate and was not
  touched.
- The `isekai-game-dev` skill's prose escape hatch ('if the target cannot be mapped to
  two axes, write the reason and take the next game') is left in place deliberately. It
  now covers the residual case the detector cannot see: a game that declares both axes
  and still has no direction to steer.")))
